Z||000000||SibERThuG||07-11-2000||12:37 AM||||Last week I was pulling into a parking lot in a light rain when my car didn't turn when the wheels did, causing my front driver's side wheel to crash into a curb. Now my front end feels "light" when I turn and my tires now squeel at a 30 mph highway on/off ramp. I used to be able to go 65mph!! I looked and nothing was broken. I was told I need an alignment and a CAMBER ADJUSTMENT? Does this sound possible/right to you guys?? What else could this be?? ||||reg||9
Z||000001||Red2m4||07-11-2000||01:09 AM||||<BR>  Ok, this is a little outside my range of expertise, but i'll give it a shot.  I *believe* that camber refers to the angle of the tire to the road.  (Normally 90*)  If you bounced off a curb, then you tire is probably at something other than 90*. If you<BR>look at it, you may be able to tell if it severly off. The squeeling is cuased by your tire riding on the sidewall, and not the tread.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||lowCG||07-11-2000||11:06 AM||||Don't want to be a downer,but it sounds like you're going to need some work,should start with an inspection on an alignment rack to see how far out it is,check for a bent wheel,and be sure to replace the wheel bearing also.||||reg||1
